WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.880
All right, my friends.

00:00.880 --> 00:05.920
So now, before we deep dive into the tools and the cool stuff we have, first to have a good understanding

00:05.920 --> 00:08.400
about what is exactly data warehouse.

00:08.440 --> 00:11.920
Why the companies try to build such a data management system.

00:11.960 --> 00:14.040
So now the question is what is a data warehouse?

00:14.080 --> 00:17.840
I will just use the definition of the father of the data warehouse.

00:17.880 --> 00:18.680
Bill Inmon.

00:18.720 --> 00:25.200
A data warehouse is a subject oriented, integrated, time variance and non-volatile collection of data

00:25.240 --> 00:28.480
designed to support the management's decision making process.

00:28.520 --> 00:30.520
Okay, I know that might be confusing.

00:30.520 --> 00:31.440
Subject oriented.

00:31.440 --> 00:37.320
It means data warehouse is always focused on a business area like the sales, customers, finance and

00:37.320 --> 00:37.720
so on.

00:37.760 --> 00:41.160
Integrated because it goes and integrates multiple source systems.

00:41.160 --> 00:45.520
Usually you build a warehouse not only for one source, but for multiple sources.

00:45.560 --> 00:46.240
Time variance.

00:46.240 --> 00:51.000
It means you can keep historical data inside the data warehouse non-volatile.

00:51.000 --> 00:55.760
It means once the data enter the data warehouse, it is not deleted or modified.

00:55.760 --> 00:58.880
So this is how Bill Inmon defined data warehouse okay.

00:58.880 --> 01:03.280
So now I'm going to show you the scenario where your company don't have a real data management.

01:03.280 --> 01:08.160
So now let's say that you have one system and you have like one data analyst has to go to the system

01:08.160 --> 01:14.450
and start collecting and extracting the data, and then he can spend days and sometimes weeks transforming

01:14.450 --> 01:16.770
the raw data into something meaningful.

01:16.770 --> 01:19.770
Then once they have their report, they're going to go and share it.

01:19.770 --> 01:23.050
And this data analyst is sharing the report using an Excel.

01:23.050 --> 01:27.610
And then you have like another source of data and you have another data analyst that she is doing.

01:27.610 --> 01:32.890
Maybe the same steps collecting the data, spending a lot of time transforming the data and then share

01:32.930 --> 01:34.490
at the end like a report.

01:34.490 --> 01:39.250
And this time she is sharing the data using PowerPoint and a third system and the same story.

01:39.250 --> 01:42.770
But this time he is sharing the data using maybe power BI.

01:42.810 --> 01:46.170
So now if the company works like this then there is a lot of issues.

01:46.210 --> 01:48.970
First, this process would take too way long.

01:49.010 --> 01:54.690
I saw a lot of scenarios where sometimes it takes weeks and even months until the employee manually

01:54.690 --> 01:56.170
generating those reports.

01:56.170 --> 01:58.090
And of course what's going to happen for the users.

01:58.130 --> 02:02.450
They are consuming multiple reports with multiple state of the data.

02:02.450 --> 02:07.690
One report is 40 days old, another 110 days, and a third one is like five days.

02:07.690 --> 02:11.610
So it's going to be really hard to make a real decision based on this structure.

02:11.650 --> 02:17.070
A manual process is always slow and stressful, and the more employees you involved in the process.

02:17.070 --> 02:23.070
The more you open the door for human errors and errors, of course, and reports leads to bad decisions.

02:23.070 --> 02:26.270
And another issue, of course, is handling the big data.

02:26.270 --> 02:31.790
If one of your sources generating like massive amounts of data, then the data analysts can struggle

02:31.830 --> 02:32.870
collecting the data.

02:32.870 --> 02:36.990
And maybe in some scenarios it will not be any more possible to get the data.

02:36.990 --> 02:42.550
So the whole process can breaks and you cannot generate any more fresh data for specific reports.

02:42.550 --> 02:48.830
And one last very big issue with that, if one of your stakeholders asks for an integrated report from

02:48.830 --> 02:55.030
multiple sources, well, good luck with that, because merging all those data manually is very chaotic,

02:55.070 --> 02:56.910
time consuming and full of risk.

02:56.910 --> 02:58.510
So this is just a picture.

02:58.510 --> 03:04.270
If a company is working without a proper data management, without a data lake, data warehouse, data

03:04.310 --> 03:05.190
lake houses.

03:05.190 --> 03:09.710
So in order to make real and good decisions, you need data management.

03:09.710 --> 03:12.390
So now let's talk about the scenario of a data warehouse.

03:12.390 --> 03:17.790
So the first thing that's going to happen is that you will not have your data team collecting manually

03:17.790 --> 03:18.430
the data.

03:18.470 --> 03:22.030
You're going to have a very important component called ETL.

03:22.190 --> 03:25.280
ETL stands for extract, transform and Load.

03:25.320 --> 03:30.200
It is a process that you do in order to extract the data from the sources, and then apply multiple

03:30.200 --> 03:35.160
transformations on those sources, and at the end it loads the data to the data warehouse.

03:35.160 --> 03:39.520
And this one can be the single point of truth for analysis and reporting.

03:39.520 --> 03:41.760
And it is called data warehouse.

03:41.760 --> 03:43.000
So now what can happen?

03:43.000 --> 03:47.160
All your reports can be consuming this single point of truth.

03:47.160 --> 03:49.600
So with that you create your multiple reports.

03:49.600 --> 03:55.800
And as well you can create integrated reports from multiple sources, not only from one single source.

03:55.800 --> 03:58.960
So now by looking to the right side it looks already organized.

03:58.960 --> 03:59.360
Right.

03:59.360 --> 04:02.080
And the whole process is completely automated.

04:02.080 --> 04:06.720
There is no more manual steps, which of course it reduces the human error.

04:06.720 --> 04:08.520
And as well it is pretty fast.

04:08.520 --> 04:14.640
So usually you can load the data from the sources until the reports in a matter of hours or sometimes

04:14.640 --> 04:15.440
in minutes.

04:15.440 --> 04:20.440
So there is no need to wait like weeks and months in order to refresh anything.

04:20.440 --> 04:25.560
And of course, the big advantage is that the data warehouse itself, it is completely integrated.

04:25.560 --> 04:31.300
So that means it goes and bring all those sources together in one place, which makes it really easier

04:31.300 --> 04:33.500
for reporting and not only integrated.

04:33.500 --> 04:35.660
You can build in the data warehouse as well.

04:35.700 --> 04:36.140
History.

04:36.140 --> 04:39.420
So we have now the possibility to access historical data.

04:39.420 --> 04:44.700
And what is also amazing is that all those reports having the same data status.

04:44.700 --> 04:49.140
So all those reports can have the same status, maybe sometimes one day old or something.

04:49.180 --> 04:54.460
And of course, if you have a modern data warehouse in cloud platforms, you can really easily handle

04:54.500 --> 04:56.100
any big data sources.

04:56.100 --> 05:00.780
So no need to panic if one of your sources is delivering massive amount of data.

05:00.820 --> 05:05.180
And of course, in order to build the data warehouse, you need different types of developers.

05:05.180 --> 05:11.300
So usually the one that builds the ETL component and the data warehouse is the data engineer.

05:11.340 --> 05:16.980
So they are the one that is accessing the sources, scripting the atls and building the database for

05:16.980 --> 05:17.900
the data warehouse.

05:17.940 --> 05:19.380
And now for the other parts.

05:19.540 --> 05:22.500
The one that is responsible for that is the data analyst.

05:22.500 --> 05:28.300
They are the one that is consuming the data warehouse, building different data models and reports,

05:28.300 --> 05:30.460
and sharing it with the stakeholders.

05:30.460 --> 05:35.500
So they are usually contacting the stakeholders, understanding the requirements and building multiple

05:35.500 --> 05:37.780
reports based on that data warehouse.

05:37.780 --> 05:43.030
So now if you have a look to those two scenarios, this is exactly why we need data management.

05:43.070 --> 05:47.670
Your data team is not wasting time and fighting with the data.

05:47.710 --> 05:51.790
They are now more organized and more focused and with like a data warehouse.

05:51.790 --> 05:58.390
And you are delivering professional and fresh reports that your company can count on in order to make

05:58.430 --> 06:00.230
good and fast decisions.

06:00.350 --> 06:03.670
So this is why you need a data management like a data warehouse.

06:03.710 --> 06:06.390
Think about data warehouse as a busy restaurant.

06:06.430 --> 06:12.110
Every day different suppliers bring in fresh ingredients vegetables, spices, meat, you name it.

06:12.150 --> 06:15.590
They don't just use it immediately and throw everything in one pot, right?

06:15.630 --> 06:21.630
They clean it, shop it, and organize everything and store each ingredients in the right place.

06:21.630 --> 06:22.910
Fridge or freezer.

06:22.950 --> 06:24.710
So this is the preparing phase.

06:24.710 --> 06:30.830
And when the order comes in, they quickly grab the prepared ingredients and create a perfect dish and

06:30.830 --> 06:33.030
then serve it to the customers of the restaurant.

06:33.030 --> 06:36.070
And this process is exactly like the data warehouse process.

06:36.070 --> 06:41.750
It is like the kitchen where the raw ingredients, your data are cleaned, sorted and stored.

06:41.750 --> 06:47.070
And when you need a report or analysis, it is ready to serve up exactly like what you need.
